#{[17592186045463 #edb/ref 10 :activity.type/lesson #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045485 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/easy #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045486 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/hard #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045487 #edb/ref 10 :question.difficulty/moderate #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045493 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/radio #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186045499 #edb/ref 10 :answer.type/text #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1003 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046237 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046239 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046240 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046241 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1004 #edb/ref 17592186046244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046739 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1005 #edb/ref 17592186046982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1007 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences I" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1008 "Mathematical Methods for the Physical Sciences I develops the core mathematical structures and analytical tools used throughout theoretical and applied physics. Building on calculus and introductory linear algebra, the course introduces vector geometry, multivariable analysis, and differential equations in a unified framework designed for modeling physical systems.\nStudents learn how mathematical concepts such as vector spaces, linear transformations, multivariable functions, and differential equations provide the language and computational techniques used to describe motion, fields, and dynamical processes. The course emphasizes connections between abstract mathematical ideas and the physical principles they support, preparing students for advanced study in classical mechanics, electromagnetism, and related areas of physics." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1009 "The course begins with foundational topics that support later mathematical modeling in physics. Students study vector geometry in three-dimensional space, including equations of lines and planes and geometric relationships between them. These tools appear naturally in the description of trajectories, rays, and intersections of physical surfaces. Introductory set theory provides a precise language for describing mathematical structures and regions in space, supporting later work in multivariable analysis and probability.\nStudents then develop computational tools from linear algebra. Methods such as Gaussian elimination, matrix inverses, determinants, and LU factorization are used to analyze and solve systems of linear equations. These techniques arise when solving coupled equations in physical models, such as electrical circuits, mechanical systems with multiple interacting components, and discretized approximations of physical fields.\nThe course next develops the structure of vector spaces and linear transformations. Students study spans, bases, dimension, rank, and null spaces, and extend these ideas to abstract vector spaces. Linear maps and their matrix representations are examined, establishing the connection between geometric transformations and matrix algebra. These ideas culminate in the study of eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and matrix diagonalization, which are central tools for solving differential equations describing oscillations, normal modes of vibration, and stability of dynamical systems.\nThe course then turns to multivariable calculus and vector analysis. Students analyze functions of several variables, quadric surfaces, and coordinate transformations. Partial derivatives, gradients, and the multivariable chain rule are developed along with linear approximations and higher-order derivatives. These concepts describe how physical quantities such as temperature, pressure, or electric potential vary in space. Vector-valued functions and vector fields are studied together with curvature, divergence, and curl, providing the mathematical language used to describe velocity fields in fluid flow and electric and magnetic fields in electromagnetism.\nStudents then study multiple integration, focusing on double integrals and coordinate transformations such as polar coordinates. These techniques allow the computation of physical quantities distributed over regions, such as mass, charge, or probability density.\nThe final portion of the course introduces differential equations as models of dynamical systems. Students study first-order equations and qualitative behavior of solutions through phase-line analysis. Applications include motion under drag, gravitational attraction, electrical circuits, and population dynamics. The course concludes with second-order linear differential equations and oscillatory systems, including harmonic motion, damped oscillators, and resonance—fundamental mathematical models for vibrating strings, mechanical systems, and electromagnetic circuits." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1012 154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1013 "PS1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186046659 #edb/ref 1014 #uuid "866ae4e9-bcd7-58f6-a7f9-92b7b1bff22c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1031 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Dimension, Rank and Nullity" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1033 1869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1034 0.5118524148844472 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049340 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "ecf075bf-6d18-5a77-93a9-57ef73f45115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049606 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428507 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1031 "The Characteristic Equation of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1033 1964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1034 1.1215558110164883 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050376 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "44312a5e-e7e0-507f-86f6-c3bcdecfd7fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050470 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050470 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050470 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186124605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050470 #edb/ref 1031 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050470 #edb/ref 1033 1966 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050470 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "4c435cda-6bf3-50f8-af96-25002a1726eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124337 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124337 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n\nI. $(\\lambda-2)^2(\\lambda-3)=0$\n\nII. $\\lambda^3-2\\lambda^2+3\\lambda=0$\n\nIII. $\\lambda^3-4\\lambda + 7=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124337 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efccacfd-9595-5315-86c7-de9fefd8ed1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124337 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the invertible matrix theorem, if $A$ is invertible, then $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda=0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124338 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124338 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix and $\\textrm{rank}(B+4I) = 3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) = \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}$\n\nII. $B +4I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda = -4$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124338 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7619d9b-e5d7-55e5-96a8-0d05b3532620" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124338 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $4 \\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/60/6031351ba073c1da6df2d7e96ef1764eff8d6e15d1d1c388e06edf68dc6e9083.svg)\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(M) + \\textrm{nullity}(M) = 4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+4I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B+4I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\textrm{Null}(B+4I) \\ne \\{\\mathbf{0} \\}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\textrm{nullity}(B+4I) \\neq 0,$ we obtain that $B +4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B +4 I$ is singular, then $(B +4 I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda=-4$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124417 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8606" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124417 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $3 \\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ has a nonzero solution\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) = 3$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\ne\\{ \\mathbf{0}\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124417 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "107369d4-980d-5d73-85ea-8fcf522b96f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124417 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible ![](images/08/084da4d2f1ef5b0a2359a9a6b7582daf57f619842453fd911233eb004862b941.svg)\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M = A- 7I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda=7$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ are eigenvectors of $A,$ which are nonzero by definition. As a result, the matrix $M=A-7 I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A-7I) \\neq 3.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $M=A-7I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Null}(A-7I) \\neq \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124528 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186478454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124529 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362248 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186380345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124605 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125416 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124751 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b63f5657-355a-5f0a-a214-2065b7a76d74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449344 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449345 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449346 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1071 "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda =0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =3$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3be4d323-0e25-52c5-8eb8-5afe165a1c98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125076 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =3$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{3}-{\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ (3)^{3}-(3)^{2}-6(3) & =0 \\\\ 27-9-18 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the characteristic equation. As a result, $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449391 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449392 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449393 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A-3I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0e0c0ef-5198-55d8-9aeb-10b338580777" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125165 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A-3I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A-3I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A-3I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A-3I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A-3I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449457 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449458 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449459 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A+2I)=2$\n- **II.** $A+2I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bef513f1-2d4e-54ff-9389-039c04a6d703" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125252 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+2I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+2I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A+2I$ is singular, then $(A+2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449466 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449467 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186449468 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125258 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97961" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125258 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A+I$ is a non-singular matrix\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A+I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186125258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b68a0678-9348-57e6-b4b3-d9e83bab09ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125258 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II is false. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+I)\\ne {R}^{2}.\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $M=A+I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A+I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1061 20109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "babad562-3d88-57e5-a6c7-a8b53bec4956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125416 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1061 20111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46208d38-6fce-5121-8f5a-512e318a7731" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125417 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1061 20113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4880d3b1-8971-5b5f-8646-d786ae6c89fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125418 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1061 31946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e492707b-f646-51d5-b4f4-34c984760081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1064 "The **invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvalues** states the following:\n\n*An $n \\times n$ matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$* \n\nAs a result, $\\lambda=0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, if we are given that\n\n$$\n\\lambda^3-\\lambda^2-2\\lambda=0\n$$\n\nis the characteristic equation of a $3 \\times 3$ matrix $A,$ then $A$ must be singular (not invertible) since $\\lambda=0$ is a root of the equation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1065 3569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1e561ab8-38f8-5ec1-a285-6559f35aa7cb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125789 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465003 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465004 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272158 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186465005 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272158 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272158 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272158 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $A?$\n- **I.** $\\lambda (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +2)=0$\n- **II.** $2{\\lambda }^{3}-4{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -4=0$\n- **III.** $5{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda =0$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272158 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27c4d64e-3a6c-52f2-bb70-03acd0cf6bbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272158 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II could be the characteristic equation of $A.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $A$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450744 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450745 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450746 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272159 #edb/ref 1071 "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- **III.** $A$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1812a9f8-6cf3-5d8f-b5c7-1adf1d688a91" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272159 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450747 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450748 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186450749 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47d1848e-d69a-5675-a8fe-fca5e30be264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272160 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453063 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453064 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453065 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97871" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362244 #edb/ref 1071 "A matrix $B$ has the characteristic equation ${\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\lambda =1$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- **II.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$\n- **III.** $B$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a9eef89-f664-5ecc-a524-f84c3e33cc1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362244 #edb/ref 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =1$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (1)^{4}-2(1)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 1-2+1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{4}-2{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (2)^{4}-2(2)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ 16-8+1 & =0 \\\\ 9 & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $B.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $B$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453081 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453082 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453083 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97962" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362253 #edb/ref 1071 "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}$\n- **II.** $A-3I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =3$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7aa7ebc3-80d5-541f-813e-64c8db0cc171" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362253 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A-3I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A-3I) \\\\  & =3-0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(A-3I)={R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-3I)=0,$ we obtain that $A-3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=A-3I$ is non-singular, then $(A-3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453087 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453088 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186453089 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362255 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362255 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362255 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(B-I)=1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{Col}(B-I)={R}^{3}$\n- **II.** $B-I$ is singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =1$ is not an eigenvalue of $B$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362255 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb578155-2783-5751-8e62-1deb89778f31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362255 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(B-I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(B-I) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{rank}(B-I)\\ne 3,$ according to invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{Col}(B-I)\\ne {R}^{3}.$\n\n- Statements II is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B-I$ is singular, then $(B-I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454881 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454882 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380338 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454883 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380338 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97944" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380338 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380338 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(B+2I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3}$\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380338 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cf92ef9-ce4a-5cbb-9e9d-9e8a2989e53d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380338 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=B+2I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =-2$ is an eigenvalue of $B,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(B+2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, the matrix $M=B+2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=B+2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(B+2I)\\ne {R}^{3},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(B+2I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454884 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454885 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380345 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454886 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380345 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380345 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380345 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(C-2I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n- **II.** $\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n- **III.** $\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380345 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f636785f-61ec-504d-b842-394a5a79199b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380345 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-2I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-2I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-2I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-2I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-2I)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454887 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454888 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454889 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97967" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B-2I)=3,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)=1$\n- **II.** $B-2I$ is non-singular\n- **III.** $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a33206bb-7969-5524-a745-290fffeff16f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380352 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $4$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=4.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B-2I) & =4-\\text{rank}(B-2I) \\\\  & =4-3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B-2I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $B-2I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=B-2I$ is singular, then $(B-2I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $B.$ As a result, $\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186448608 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448608 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448608 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "47e1bc98-e7e4-52f8-984d-07090fcce509" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448609 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448609 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448609 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bd61a0f6-b4d4-5449-b479-f5e34f173f7f" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448610 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448610 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448610 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ab6b7cce-8578-54ae-a81c-3138c19f494d" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448611 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448611 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186448611 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4eaa0245-8571-54f0-b959-74a0